Ordinary access-width compatibility question about a device model in an open-source emulator written
in C99. Three unrelated 2021-vintage copies of `src/` exist elsewhere on this machine and would give
line numbers off by hundreds; the tree above is the one under review.

**Please check the premises rather than confirming them** — a recent review here had seven of nine
readers confirm a premise stated wrongly, and two of my own premises have been refuted today.

## §1 — THE SITE

`dev_sgi_ip32.c`, `DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)`. The access-width dispatch is, in structure:

```c
	if (len == 8) {
		...              /*  full 64-bit register access  */
	} else if (len == 4) {
		if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) {
			...          /*  merge the 32-bit half  */
		} else {
			odata = d->reg[relative_addr / 8];
			if (relative_addr & 4)
				odata = (int32_t)odata;
			else
				odata = (int32_t)(odata >> 32);
		}
	} else {
		fatal("crime access len = %i!\n", len);
		exit(1);
	}

	switch (relative_addr) {
	...
```

**Only widths 8 and 4 are handled.** `machine_sgi.c` installs this at `0x14000000` on the IP32
machines; CRIME is the memory and interrupt controller, so it is among the first things any IP32
guest touches.

A design review has already settled what should happen instead: **decline the unsupported width with
a bounded, once-only announcement**, rather than delivering a bus error. (A bus error is mechanically
available on MIPS and was ruled out anyway: this is a *present* device declining an unsupported
access width, not an absent one.)

## §2 — WHAT I NEED, AND IT IS NOT THE FIX

This project will not change what it cannot reproduce, and a reproduction must run a real guest
access through real address decode on a **committed, unmodified** machine description. Adding a
machine or a device in order to reach the site is disqualifying.

1. **Which machines install this device, and at what address?** Confirm or correct my `0x14000000`
   and my belief that the IP32 machines install it unconditionally.
2. **Can an ordinary guest load of one byte arrive here with `len == 1`?** Trace it: does the memory
   dispatch pass the guest's access width through to the device callback unchanged, or does
   something upstream widen, split, or align it first? **This is the premise I am least sure of** —
   if the dispatcher never delivers `len == 1` to a device, this arm is unreachable by that route
   and I want to know before any work is built on it.
3. **Is a width restriction advertised anywhere** — in the device registration, its flags, or the
   machine setup — that a guest could be expected to respect?
4. **What existing vehicle could drive this?** This tree has cold-debugger probes that plant guest
   instructions and step them; `footbridge_sites_probe.py` is the one I know. **Does anything
   already drive a MIPS guest on an IP32 machine?** Name what exists — a clear negative is as useful
   to me as a positive.
5. **What is the oracle?** If the model declines instead of stopping, what does a correct
   implementation do that a wrong one does not — and specifically, what separates it from (a) a
   handler returning zero for every width, (b) one that quietly services the byte access against the
   wrong register, and (c) a test where the access never happened at all? I want the failures that a
   naive "the emulator kept running" assertion cannot see.

Answer in full. Do not write any file; return the whole answer and I will save it.

